{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>The after-growth of your harvest</b> — even if you did not sow and it grew from seed that fell at the time of harvest, and it is called ספיח (after-growth) — <b>you shall not reap</b> — to retain it for yourself like any other harvest, but it shall be ownerless for all. <b>Your untrimmed vines</b> — which you held back and separated from other people and did not declare ownerless — <b>you shall not gather</b> — you are not gathering from what is ownerless; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:5). And the intent of the Rav in this is that he holds that one who guards his field and its produce in the seventh year — the produce is not forbidden. And so he wrote in his commentary in tractate Yevamot (122a, s.v. של עזיקה) and in tractate Sukkah (39a, s.v. אבל), and this matter is likewise the law according to the Torah with clear proofs. And that which is taught in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, section 3), \"But the grapes of your untrimmed vines you shall not gather\" — from what is guarded in the land you may not gather, but you may gather from what is ownerless — the Rav will explain to say: grapes of nezirut, you shall not gather them in their nezirut, but you shall declare everything ownerless and gather together with the poor. And the correct interpretation of the verse is that \"grapes of your untrimmed vines (נְזִירֶךָ)\" is like \"the after-growth of your harvest (סְפִיחַ קְצִירְךָ),\" and \"you shall not gather (לֹא תִבְצֹר)\" is like \"you shall not reap (לֹא תִקְצֹר),\" for the subject throughout the entire passage duplicates the ruling in both cases: it warns concerning the field and warns concerning the vineyard — \"you shall sow your field\" and \"you shall prune your vineyard\" (Leviticus 25:3); \"your field you shall not sow and your vineyard you shall not prune\" (Leviticus 25:4); and likewise \"Six years you shall sow your land and the seventh year you shall let it rest and leave it; so shall you do to your vineyard\" (Exodus 23:11). And the meaning of the verse is that what grows on its own in the field without plowing and deliberate sowing is called סְפִיחַ קָצִיר, because it is appended (נִסְפָּח) to the harvest of the year that passed, derived from \"and they shall be joined (וְנִסְפְּחוּ) to the house of Jacob\" (Isaiah 14:1). And the vine that one did not work and did not hoe and did not prune is called נָזִיר, because one held it back (הִזִּירוֹ) and separated it from oneself as though it is not one's own, derived from \"who separate themselves (נָזֹרוּ) from me with their idols\" (Ezekiel 14:5) — they separated themselves from me, and likewise \"and they shall separate themselves (וְיִנָּזְרוּ) from the holy things of the children of Israel\" (Leviticus 22:2). And Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Leviticus 25:5) שִׁבְקָךְ — you left them to thorns and wild growth. And it is possible that the custom in Israel was that a vineyard which is abandoned — one does not prune it or work it and thorns and wild growth grow up — they would call it נָזִיר, meaning the vineyard of the nazirite, because the nazirite is forbidden wine and fresh grapes and dried grapes and does not work his vineyard. For thus they also called long hair by the name of the nazirite: \"Cut off your nazirite-locks (נִזְרֵךְ) and cast them away\" (Jeremiah 7:29). And this is the meaning of \"grapes of your untrimmed vines (נְזִירֶךָ).\" The general principle is that these are what grow without working the vineyard. Now the verse states that the after-growth that grows on its own in the field — you shall not reap it, and the grapes that grow in the vineyard without work — you shall not gather them. And the meaning of the prohibitions is: you shall not reap them you alone for your own needs, and you shall not gather them for yourself, but it shall be a sabbath year for the land from sowing and pruning, and \"the sabbath of the land\" shall be — all that it produces in its rest from the after-growth and the untrimmed growth — for all of you together for food, for you and for your poor and for the beast and for the animal.\n\nAnd now I will return to explain the baraitot taught in Torat Kohanim on this matter, because they are misleading. It is taught there (chapter 4, section 5) on the verse \"If we do not sow and do not gather our produce\" (Leviticus 25:20): if they do not sow, what do they gather? Rabbi Akiva said: from here they derived support for after-growth (סְפִיחִין) that they are forbidden in the seventh year. And there is a difficulty raised against this in the Gemara in the chapter Makom SheNahagu (Pesachim 51a), and it is taught in Torat Kohanim: but the Sages say: after-growth is not forbidden by Torah law but only by rabbinic decree. If so, why is it said \"If we do not sow and do not gather\"? You would say to us: do not sow; and what we gather and do not bring in for storage, you say to us: clear it out (בַעֲרוּהוּ). What do we eat from the clearing-out (בִעוּר) onward? — end of quotation. According to the Sages, then, the prohibition of after-growth is only by their decree, meaning that they decreed to forbid them entirely on account of suspicion, lest one sow and say they are after-growth. And therefore they said in the Jerusalem Talmud (Bava Batra 5:1): Rabbi Yannai said, all after-growth is forbidden except what grows in a fallow field, in a plowed field, in a vineyard field, and in a sown field. Fallow field — because he pays no attention to it. Plowed field — because it requires preparation of the field. Vineyard field — so as not to forbid his vineyard. Sown field — because he does not want its continued existence. And from this dispute it is taught there in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, section 3): \"The after-growth of your harvest you shall not reap\" — from here they derived support for after-growth that it should be forbidden in the seventh year. They taught their mishnah according to the words of Rabbi Akiva, that \"you shall not reap\" is an absolute prohibition. But according to the Sages: you shall not make them a harvest for yourself, and you shall not make them a vintage for yourself, but conduct yourself with them according to the practice of ownerlessness. And likewise \"and do not gather our produce\" (Leviticus 25:20) — we shall not gather into our houses the fruit of the tree and the after-growth, for if one declares them ownerless for the poor and for the beast and for the animal and performs bi'ur, we cannot sustain ourselves from them.\n\nNow these forbidden after-growths are those that grow during the forbidden time: in the case of grain, all that reached a third of its growth in the seventh year; and likewise vegetables — all that grew in the seventh year are forbidden by Torah law according to Rabbi Akiva, and according to the Sages they are forbidden by rabbinic decree, except for what grows in those fields mentioned in the Jerusalem Talmud that we cited above. But vegetables that grew in the sixth year and were gathered in the seventh year have the sanctity of seventh-year produce in all its laws — for loss, for offerings, for commerce, and for bi'ur — even though they grew entirely in the sixth year, because we follow the gathering (לְקִיטָה) for vegetables, both for tithes and for the seventh year. But they do not have the status of after-growth, for they are not after-growth of the seventh year since they grew in the sixth year. And even if they continued to grow in the seventh year, they do not have the law of after-growth. And concerning them we learned (Shevi'it 8:4): One who says to the laborer, \"Take this coin and gather vegetables for me today,\" etc. And likewise we learned (Shevi'it 7:3): One may not gather vegetables of the seventh year and sell them in the market, but one may gather, and his son may sell through him. And likewise we learned (Shevi'it 9:1): The wild figs and the christs-thorn and the entire mishnah until \"and they may be purchased from any person in the seventh year\" — that there is nothing like them that is guarded.\n\nAnd some say that these species mentioned and all similar to them that people do not normally sow, but they grow on their own in the mountains and along the rivers — there is no prohibition of after-growth at all, even though they grew in the seventh year, because they decreed only on sown species, lest one come to sow them. And this likewise appears from the Gemara of the Jerusalem Talmud, and this is the meaning of after-growth that the Sages mentioned everywhere. And likewise by Torah law one may not guard one's field and lock it before the poor in the seventh year, even if one wants to declare it ownerless at the time of gathering, but the field must be all year available and ownerless for the poor. Thus it is taught in the Mekhilta (Mishpatim, 20:11): \"And the poor of your people shall eat\" (Exodus 23:11) — and for what reason did the Torah say this? Is it not that the poor should eat it? Behold, I can bring it in and distribute it to the poor! Scripture says: \"the seventh year you shall let it rest and leave it (וּנְטַשְׁתָּהּ)\" — this teaches that one makes breaches in it. Except that the Sages fenced this off on account of tikkun olam. And the fence that the Sages made is that one may not make breaches in the field and in the vineyard, but not that one may guard one's field and bring in the produce thereby transgressing Torah law. And not only that, but they decreed not to purchase produce from one who guards and not from one suspected of this. This is what we learned (Shevi'it 9:1): \"And they may be purchased from any person in the seventh year\" — that there is nothing like them that is guarded. And they said in tractate Sukkah (Sukkah 39a): One may not give seventh-year produce money to an am ha-aretz more than the sustenance of three meals. In what case is this said? When purchasing from what is ownerless. But when purchasing from what is guarded, even half an issar is forbidden.\n\nThe reason is that one who purchases from what is ownerless — meaning things that people normally bring from ownerless property, or where it is known that he brought them from an ownerless place — it is permitted to purchase from him, except that if he charges more than the cost of his meal for that day, there is suspicion concerning an am ha-aretz lest he engage in commerce with them. But when one purchases things that are normally preserved — even for half an issar, there is suspicion lest he guarded them, and it is forbidden to purchase from him. But when purchasing in small change for less than half an issar, they did not decree, because people normally gather them everywhere, even in other years of the week. And this is what they said (Yevamot 122a): A gentile who was selling produce in the market and said, \"These produce items — these are orlah, these are guarded\" — he said nothing; he only intended to enhance his merchandise. And Rashi explained in the name of the early commentators: from an orchard that is fortified and fenced around, and it is a seventh year, and if they were to believe him it would be forbidden to purchase from him in a city whose majority is Israelite — lest he be a tenant farmer for an Israelite and guarding his field, or there is suspicion lest an Israelite gathered and is selling, or the produce belongs to an Israelite and he is selling through him, or perhaps a gentile cannot acquire in the land of Israel to negate the laws of the seventh year, and they decreed upon him as upon an Israelite.\n\nNevertheless, the Sages did not forbid the produce of a guarded field to everyone, but only that one should not purchase them from the one who guards. Therefore they taught in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, section 3): \"But the grapes of your untrimmed vines you shall not gather\" — from what is guarded in the land you may not gather, but you may gather from what is ownerless. \"You shall not gather\" — in the manner of those who gather vintage; from here they said: figs of the seventh year — one may not cut them with a harvesting tool, and one may not tread grapes in the winepress, but one treads them in a trough. Its meaning is: since it is not written עִנְבֵי הַנָּזִיר \"grapes of the nazirite\" but \"נְזִירֶךָ\" — to derive: you shall not gather them in the manner of your nazirite, but you may gather them with the poor in the manner that they gather — meaning one may not keep them for oneself, but one may gather them with the poor as ownerless, and conduct oneself with them also in the manner that the poor conduct themselves in a trough.\n\nAnd it is taught there (ibid., section 5): \"And the sabbath of the land shall be for you for food\" — from what is abandoned in the land you may eat, but you may not eat from what is guarded. From here they said (Shevi'it 4:2): A field that was fertilized — Beit Shammai say: one may not eat its fruit in the seventh year, and Beit Hillel say: one may eat. That is, since the Torah said that what is abandoned shall be for us for food and not what is guarded, Beit Shammai said that the produce itself is forbidden, and Beit Hillel hold that the produce is not forbidden, for the verse came only to forbid us from guarding, so that the produce should be available to us from what is abandoned. And likewise the matter that they said — that one may not cut the figs with a harvesting tool and may not tread the grapes in the winepress — is a fence and an asmakhta from their words, lest one come to guarding and gathering produce and robbing the poor. And this is the meaning taught here in these baraitot, and it is a good and clear path according to the sugyot in the Babylonian and Jerusalem Talmud. And the discerning scholar will understand and find."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase פֵּרוֹת הַשָּׂדֶה הַמְשֻׁמָּר appears to refer generally to produce of a guarded field; I rendered it literally without resolving whether Ramban means specifically field-produce as opposed to tree fruit."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:24:55.288248834Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}